{"date": "2026-10-24", "post_id": 1502171, "title": "Rosetta's Selfie", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-24-rosettas-selfie/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://blogs.esa.int/rosetta/2014/10/14/mission-selfie-from-16-km/\">This Rosetta spacecraft selfie</a> was snapped on October 7th, 2014. At the time the <a href=\"https://rosetta.esa.int/\">comet-chasing spacecraft</a> was about 472 million kilometers from planet Earth, but only 16 kilometers from the surface of <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2021-november-13-rosettas-comet-in-gemini/\">comet 67P/Churyumov-Gerasimenko</a>. Looming beyond the spacecraft near the top of the frame, dust and gas stream away from the comet's curious <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2014-august-7-rosettas-rendezvous/\">double-lobed nucleus</a>. Bright sunlight glints off one of Rosetta's 14 meter long solar arrays. In fact, two exposures, one short and one long, were combined to record the dramatic high contrast scene using the CIVA camera system on Rosetta's still-attached <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philae_(spacecraft)\">Philae lander</a>. Its chosen <a href=\"https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2016DPS....4810101T/abstract\">primary landing site</a> was on the visible hemisphere of the smaller lobe of the nucleus. This was the last image from Philae's camera before the lander separated from Rosetta on November 12. Shortly after separation Philae took another image looking back toward the orbiter and <a href=\"http://www.esa.int/spaceinvideos/Videos/2014/09/Philae_s_descent_and_science_on_the_surface\">began its descent</a> to the nucleus of the comet.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong>dark matters<br>", "credit": "<a href=\"http://rosetta.esa.int/\">ESA/Rosetta/Philae/CIV</a>A", "copyright": "<a href=\"http://rosetta.esa.int/\">ESA/Rosetta/Philae/CIV</a>A", "alt": "Image of a Rosetta spacecraft solar array in foreground and the nucleus of comet 67P in background.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-24-rosettas-selfie/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/ESA_Rosetta_Philae_CIVA_141007.png?w=1024&h=1024&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 October 24 - Rosetta&#039;s Selfie</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-24-rosettas-selfie/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: This Rosetta spacecraft selfie was snapped on October 7th, 2014. At the time the comet-chasing spacecraft was about 472 million kilometers from planet Earth, but only 16 kilometers from the surface of comet 67P/Churyumov-Gerasimenko. Looming beyond the spacecraft near the top of the frame, dust and gas stream away from the comet&#039;s curious double-lobed nucleus. Bright sunlight glints off one of Rosetta&#039;s 14 meter long solar arrays. In fact, two exposures, one short and one long, were combined to record the dramatic high contrast scene using the CIVA camera system on Rosetta&#039;s still-attached Philae lander. Its chosen primary landing site was on the visible hemisphere of the smaller lobe of the nucleus. This was the last image from Philae&#039;s camera before the lander separated from Rosetta on November 12. Shortly after separation Philae took another image looking back toward the orbiter and began its descent to the nucleus of the comet.Tomorrow&#039;s picture: dark matters\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"rosetta&#039;s selfie\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"Rosetta&#039;s Selfie\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-24-rosettas-selfie/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/ESA_Rosetta_Philae_CIVA_141007.png?w=1024&#038;h=1024&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/ESA_Rosetta_Philae_CIVA_141007.png?w=1024&#038;h=1024&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"Image of a Rosetta spacecraft solar array in foreground and the nucleus of comet 67P in background.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-10-24T00:05:00-04:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-10-23T18:25:18-04:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"Rosetta&#039;s Selfie\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/ESA_Rosetta_Philae_CIVA_141007.png?w=1024&#038;h=1024&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 October 24<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/ESA_Rosetta_Philae_CIVA_141007.png\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/ESA_Rosetta_Philae_CIVA_141007.png\" alt=\"Image of a Rosetta spacecraft solar array in foreground and the nucleus of comet 67P in background.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> Rosetta&#039;s Selfie </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"http://rosetta.esa.int/\">ESA/Rosetta/Philae/CIV</a>A\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://blogs.esa.int/rosetta/2014/10/14/mission-selfie-from-16-km/\">This Rosetta spacecraft selfie</a> was snapped on October 7th, 2014. At the time the <a href=\"https://rosetta.esa.int/\">comet-chasing spacecraft</a> was about 472 million kilometers from planet Earth, but only 16 kilometers from the surface of <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2021-november-13-rosettas-comet-in-gemini/\">comet 67P/Churyumov-Gerasimenko</a>. Looming beyond the spacecraft near the top of the frame, dust and gas stream away from the comet's curious <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2014-august-7-rosettas-rendezvous/\">double-lobed nucleus</a>. Bright sunlight glints off one of Rosetta's 14 meter long solar arrays. In fact, two exposures, one short and one long, were combined to record the dramatic high contrast scene using the CIVA camera system on Rosetta's still-attached <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philae_(spacecraft)\">Philae lander</a>. Its chosen <a href=\"https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2016DPS....4810101T/abstract\">primary landing site</a> was on the visible hemisphere of the smaller lobe of the nucleus. This was the last image from Philae's camera before the lander separated from Rosetta on November 12. Shortly after separation Philae took another image looking back toward the orbiter and <a href=\"http://www.esa.int/spaceinvideos/Videos/2014/09/Philae_s_descent_and_science_on_the_surface\">began its descent</a> to the nucleus of the comet.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong>dark matters<br>\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-23-andromeda-in-southern-spring/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nJerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti, Robert Nemiroff, Keighley Rockcliffe<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261024/html"}